# Articles

Apply when editing **reader-facing articles** (essays under topic folders, `blog/*.md`). **Not** investigations, `page.md` indices, or dossiers unless asked.

**Full canon:** [`docs/instructions/ARTICLES.md`](../../docs/instructions/ARTICLES.md)

## Compact

- Links: Substack URL when the target essay has a `**Substack:**` post; otherwise **`https://paradigmthreat.net/...`**. Images always on **`https://paradigmthreat.net/...`**.
- Voice: **chronicle / documentary** — who did what, in order; **`I`** only for personal witness; no poetry, no manifesto cadence, no “this essay argues,” no **ordering the reader around** (**“Stop treating… Treat them as…,”** **“Read X as Y,”** **“Hunt the… Do not wait…”**). No **you** addressed at the reader, including “You cannot.” Say who, or state the fact. **You** only inside a verbatim quote, a film line, or a title. Do not rank a source (“the videos are the record people actually watch”). Say the fact, or cut the sentence. State the claim; the reader decides.
- Prefer Ari’s spoken wording from chat; do not upgrade it into verse. Same bar for comment replies: [plain-spoken prose](plain-spoken-prose.mdc).
- **Not his words:** do not call a public line a “sentence” (a cover is a **narrative**; what he said is a **statement**) unless Ari used “sentence.” Name the person and the party. Do not swap Iranian and Israeli for “foreign influence”; comma that aside. One cite carries the claim — do not restate it and stack more figures. A reason and what follows are one thought (**however** … **so**), and “at this point” needs “in the timeline.” Keep “Surprisingly” when that is the relation. Specimen: `governance/war/world-war-3-anyway.md`, 20 Nov 2026.
- **`> **TL;DR:**` is the default**, after the hook and before the first `##`. Skip it only when Ari asks to skip it. Thesis in plain speech, no links, no section list. The opening itself is not a second summary.
- **Headings name the section.** Ordinary words for what it is about. A mood or one adjective is not enough when a stranger could take it for something else. Bad: “Lesser known.” Good: “Lesser known phrases.”
- **Corpus pictures, without being asked.** Search dossiers, timeline chapters, and sibling articles and link a picture that shows the thing that paragraph is about. More than three in a normal article, counting the hero. Not every section. A short article is not padded. A paragraph of prose sits between pictures. Skip a file if either of the two latest other reader articles already uses it, or if it already appears in four reader articles. If nothing qualifies, tell Ari which section should have a generated or found picture. Do not generate it until he says yes. Canon: [`ARTICLES.md` → Shape step 4](../../docs/instructions/ARTICLES.md#shape-how-we-open-and-move).
- **No desk scene, no job-title caption.** Do not open on the author sitting down. Do not glue a job to a name. Welles is the backup, and only when he did not give the words. Bad: “This morning I sat down because Vladimir Putin told the filmmaker Emir Kusturica that Moscow is the Third Rome, and that a fourth there shall not be.” Good: “Putin told Emir Kusturica that Moscow is the Third Rome, and that a fourth there shall not be. It inspired me to write an article about the dangers of true history.” Specimen: `history/chronology/weep-not-for-memory.md`, 21 Nov 2026.
- **No one-line statement:** a body paragraph of one sentence is an LLM artifact. Join it to the paragraph it belongs to, or cut it. Keep it when Ari said that sentence, or told the draft to say it. His two-sentence beat stays (“A Democratic Congress was the bet. A majority under the filibuster was not the restoration the Democrats were waiting for.”).
- Proof density and **Limits** live in **`investigations/`** dossiers — articles summarize and link.
- Prefer cross-linking other **articles**; link dossiers only as companion receipts.
- **Claim fact-check on revise:** check each citation and each knock-downable argument (seat counts, thresholds, dates, ratios, who said it), not the essay as a mood. A recent cite with no day and year reads as if it has always been true. Put the date on the sentence. Do not invent one. A live link used unclearly is the same fail: a stranger could hear agreement the pages do not share, a wider count than the page gives, or proof of the neighboring sentence. Author and model both get these wrong. Investigations may stay wrong and open. Articles ship polished. Body states the correct fact once; the check note goes in **`## LLM comments`** or the dossier. Canon: [`ARTICLES.md` → Claim fact-check](../../docs/instructions/ARTICLES.md#claim-fact-check-required-on-revise).
- **Sentence bridging:** the fix when a fact-check finds a cite used unclearly. Name said. Name said. is not a relation. If the next sentence is a different speaker or a different claim, the sentence says how it sits against the one before it. **However** when they diverge. **And** or **the same line** when they agree. No extra word when it is just the next fact. Do not add a caption that explains the bridge. A real **however** stays; the canned “not A, it is B” still goes. Canon: [`ARTICLES.md` → Voice → Sentence bridging](../../docs/instructions/ARTICLES.md#voice-who-we-are-writing-for).
- Run **`npm run autogen`** after substantive edits.
- Tone model when narrating: [`governance/war/the-great-depression-was-planned.md`](../../governance/war/the-great-depression-was-planned.md).
- **No correction archaeology in article bodies:** state the correct fact once; never ship “— not X”, “SRT-corrected”, or other agent-mistake scars. Hygiene belongs in the companion investigation. See [articles-no-correction-meta](articles-no-correction-meta.mdc) and ARTICLES.md drafting-process meta.
